  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000121205,
      "SummaryText": "Blue Ridge Solvents and Coatings (BRSC) manufacturers paint and paint related material, recycles waste for solvent recovery and distributes products to municipal and industrial water and wastewater purification facilities in VA and NC. The Facility repackages a variety of chemicals and warehouses many others.\n\nBRSC operates approximately 10 hours per day and has 24 hour surveillance. It employs fourteen including 4 highly trained hazmat drivers. Safe handling and shipment is critical for our employees, our customers, and the surrounding community. The proximity to the HENRY crossroads area requires a level of care even if the risk of off-site effects are extremely unlikely.\n\nChlorine, which is toxic even in low concentrations with prolonged exposure, is critical to providing safe water for many localities in VA and NC. In the past twenty three years, there have been no facility incidents involving a reportable release of this chemical.\n\nThe chlorine cylinders are received from the supplier, leak checked, and stored in an isolated, secured storage room. The product is liquefied gas under low pressure (25-125 psi). The cylinders are DOT specification heavy-walled cylinders that are not opened or modified by BRSC. They are built to withstand normal and accident conditions incident to transportation. Our supplier inspects the cylinders each time they are filled and hydrostatically tests every 5 years to 500 psi. Chlorine Institute training materials are used and are the source of the written procedures, Safety Data Sheets and Safety checklists that are used by the supplier and BRSC.\n\nSince there is no inter-container transfer, the only threat of release from these robust transportation cylinders would be from a release from the fusible valve plug that activates at 160 degrees Fahrenheit. In addition, trace leaks may result from faulty supplier procedures. The isolation of the cylinders from flammable materials, inspection of valve seals, and leak check inspections reduc"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000121205,
      "SummaryText": "e these possibilities. \n\nAll employees have received training on the standard operating procedure to follow if a chlorine leak were to occur and evacuation drills are held regularly. Coordination and training with the local Fire Department is maintained and is backed up by the Jones Chemical Response team  from Charlotte, NC. \n\nBRSC also provides the LEPC with Tier II notification of inventory annually. It has a Chlorine Institute Emergency Kit-A on site and has provided safety and emergency response assistance to operators and fire departments in its distribution area. Finally, the company is also a direct subscriber with CHEMTREC.\n\nNote to EPA:  As the operator we have held that the isolation of the cylinders that contain toxics not flammables and construction of these  DOT cylinders makes these cylinders not subject to the co-located guidance concerns . Rev032918"
    }
  ]
